{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"vanitied-tall-crustacean","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Cut the cost of obtaining the tickets store, and fix the fail-open bootstrap that silently\nproduces an empty divergent store.\n\nMeasured facts and the corrected premises: session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4**\n(dinky-frowsy-woodborer). Sibling epic: **0303-692c** (censual-organic-mouflon). Umbrella:\n**1270-b740**.\n\n## Context\n\nA fresh full clone of `tickets` costs **1.04 GiB / 59s wall / 319s CPU**. Measured against GitHub,\n`--filter=blob:none` costs **68.7 MiB / 14.3s** — a ~15x reduction. In CI the tickets objects arrive\nin the `actions/checkout` step, measured at **237 s** on run 30494781536, versus **2 s** for the\nmanual worktree mount that follows.\n\nSeparately, P1 bug **01e8-302b** (bilingual-capitalist-caecilian) is open: in Claude Code's web\nenvironment the tickets fetch timed out through the agent git proxy and `rebar init` **silently\nbootstrapped a fresh empty store**, diverging from `origin/tickets` so every read returned `[]` and\nnew writes landed on a branch that cannot fast-forward back.\n\n## Approach and why this one\n\n**rebar cannot filter a clone it does not perform.** `grep -rn 'git clone' src/` yields only a\ndocstring; every rebar fetch is post-clone, and `init.py:375` runs only inside `if remote:` — i.e.\nonly once `origin/tickets` already resolves, by which point the objects are local. So the size lever\nis the `actions/checkout` step (which has supported a `filter:` input since v4.1.0) plus documented\nclone guidance — not rebar-side fetch flags. This is a deliberate narrowing after two earlier\ndesigns were measured to be inert.\n\n`blob:none` is safe for rebar's own access patterns: whole-store `verify-identity` in a blobless\nclone fetched **0 bytes** and ran *faster* than in a full clone (3.75s vs 3.98s), because its scan is\na pure tree-level diff and event/keyring bodies are read from the **working tree**\n(`verify_authorship.py:371`, `authorship.py:119-128`). The accepted cost is stated rather than\nwaved away: `git blame` over historical trees runs on the bug-close path\n(`metrics/blame.py:69` <- `derive_caused_by` <- `transition_close.py:513`).\n\nAnd a faster fetch does **not** fix the P1 — it only lowers the probability. The mechanism is the\nfail-open fallthrough to `worktree add --orphan`, which is fixed directly.\n\n## Scope (children)\n\n1. **B3** set `merge.ours.driver=true` in the reconciler workflow (prerequisite for B1)\n2. **B1** add `filter: blob:none` to the `actions/checkout` steps that mount tickets\n3. **B2** fail closed instead of orphan-bootstrapping an empty store (fixes P1 01e8-302b)\n4. **B4** clone guidance doc + make `derive_caused_by` abort on any per-path blame failure\n5. **B5** add `--no-renames` and `-c log.showSignature=false` to the two authorship resolvers\n6. **B6** delete `.bridge_state.bak-retarget` (33,025,572 bytes sitting in the tree at HEAD)\n\n## Compatibility and rollback\n\nEvery child is independently revertible and none changes a persisted format. B3 must land **before**\nB1: `reconcile-bridge.yml` uses `fetch-depth: 0` precisely because shallow history breaks merge-base,\nand `blob:none` keeps commits but removes merge-base **blobs** which the reconverge path\nmaterializes — with `merge.ours.driver` undefined on the runner, `.bridge_state/* merge=ours`\nsilently degrades to a 3-way text merge of an 18 MB JSON file. B6 must land before epic\n0303-692c's reclaim so the strip does not retain those blobs.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A controlled **before/after byte measurement** (pack size, or `GIT_TRACE_PACKET` received\n      bytes) of a fresh CI checkout with and without the filter shows the expected reduction, **and**\n      the subsequent manual tickets fetch is confirmed to inherit\n      `remote.origin.partialclonefilter`. Must NOT be a config-presence assertion: git writes that\n      key even when the server ignored the filter (reproduced). Tier: CI job measurement.\n- [ ] A reconverge against a diverged remote succeeds on a blobless runner (proves B3 landed before\n      B1 and the merge driver is defined).\n- [ ] With a reachable remote advertising `tickets`, a simulated fetch failure causes a **non-zero\n      exit** and **no orphan branch is created**. Tier: CLI subprocess against a real temporary\n      remote. This is the RED case for P1 01e8-302b.\n- [ ] `derive_caused_by` returns `None` when blame fails on **any** path, rather than tallying a\n      partial path set and electing a culprit that would not have won over the full set (which would\n      write a wrong `caused_by` link). Tier: unit test injecting a per-path failure, asserting no\n      link event is written.\n- [ ] With `log.showSignature=true` **and signed commits** in the fixture repo, `resolve_event_commit`\n      and `resolve_position_commit` return a valid 40-hex SHA. The signing is load-bearing: with\n      `--format=%H` over unsigned commits the setting emits no extra lines, so an unsigned fixture\n      makes this vacuously green. Tier: unit against a real temporary git repo.\n- [ ] `.bridge_state.bak-retarget` is absent from the tree at HEAD and working-tree size drops by\n      >=33 MB.\n- [ ] The clone-guidance doc exists and states the measured numbers (full 1.04 GiB / 59s vs\n      blob:none 68.7 MiB / 14.3s) and the accepted `git blame` degradation.","id":"42eb-5789-c798-4dfc","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Cut tickets-store clone cost via CI partial clone + fail closed instead of bootstrapping an empty store"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785372080361335001,"uuid":"45bd2d68-d9d2-4ea9-858b-b7766f4b623d"}